#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096428 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62c34646-f7cf-5ffd-b957-4151bdbb5e44" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Left Riemann Sum in Sigma Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "87747005-ce77-5ec6-9f44-af074633aa98" :question/math-academy-id "e-401" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=4-x^2$ between $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a left Riemann sum, as shown below. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?\n\n![](images/b5/b517da6e730ac50341b133a60ae8ab99cfed7b2ce92638e6028bb3fac6fbf9da.png)" :question/worked-solution "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_k = a + k\\Delta x$ with $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we see that there are $n=3$ rectangles. The endpoints indicate that $a=-1$ and $b=2,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-(-1)}{3} = \\dfrac 3 3 = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(a+kΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{3-1}f(-1+k\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}f(k-1) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}(4-(k-1)^{2}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}(4-{k}^{2}+2k-1) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}(3+2k-{k}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096429 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d01d6ed-1c25-5170-a1ad-7a4fa0103db7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Right Riemann Sum in Sigma Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "af666c8f-dfb7-548e-a8f1-1922d4c2fdc5" :question/math-academy-id "e-402" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{ x}{2}\\right)$ between $x=0$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $3$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n f(x_i)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_i = a + i\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nNote that here we are using the index $i$ instead of $k.$ It doesn't matter which letter we use for the counter.\n\nWe're told that there are $n=3$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-0}{3} = \\dfrac{3}{3} = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}f(0+i\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}f(i) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{i}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d01d6ed-1c25-5170-a1ad-7a4fa0103db7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Right Riemann Sum in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b905cd6e-ce52-52c8-9a4a-b6ea3feabbf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b905cd6e-ce52-52c8-9a4a-b6ea3feabbf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318183} :answer-field/id #uuid "97d92250-02b3-5131-88bc-cb707adc8d67" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1af811e2-d217-57d3-a9b2-d6a86ea6f17b" :question/math-academy-id "q-345052" :question/problem "![](images/f3/f321b063e3bc76eeb5b426d00cbf94451a06cec928008eaadc965547691c412a.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y=4+x$ between $x=0,$ $x=4$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a right Riemann sum, as shown above. What is the expression for the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $i.$*\n\n${S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}($\n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we see that there are $n=4$ rectangles. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=4,$ so the step size is \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-0}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}f(0+i\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}f(i) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}(4+i).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62c34646-f7cf-5ffd-b957-4151bdbb5e44" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Left Riemann Sum in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2858820-0886-5f21-935d-6d8d793f9364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∑_(i = 0)^(2)\\sqrt{i - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095551} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ed1402-9ed7-5f03-a7a6-1b4955577c18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∑_(i = 1)^(3)\\sqrt{i + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095552} {:answer/id #uuid "0068062b-f325-5314-99d6-2d7fc8c369d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∑_(i = 0)^(2)\\sqrt{i + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095553} {:answer/id #uuid "28e394e2-ca7b-5b78-9d0e-664fd606743f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∑_(i = 0)^(2)\\sqrt{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095554} {:answer/id #uuid "566cf112-3e87-5680-9076-4e2e5bb5025b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∑_(i = 0)^(2)\\sqrt{i + 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095555}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c8832846-ab8f-51d9-b35e-38e81a04d8b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095550}] :question/id #uuid "7a82c733-fd67-5ad0-94b7-aa145f6ffd91" :question/math-academy-id "q-49264" :question/problem "![](images/35/35b881e8bb04eb192967ebc94945850fb0450012675369facd4efb41c611aaab.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y = \\sqrt{x}$ between $x = 1$, $x = 4$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a left Riemann sum, as shown above. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?"}]}
